      "text": "What is Iterator\n===============",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is Iterator\n===============",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wmWAkGoRyh5B0L4nc3sgy",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 264,
      "y": 532,
      "width": 660,
      "height": 7675,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 197999157,
      "version": 786,
      "versionNonce": 900777237,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789631099725,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "\n\n## 1. What is an Iterator?\n\n* `Iterator` is an interface used to traverse elements of a\ncollection one by one.\n* It provides methods such as:\n\n  * `hasNext()`\n  * `next()`\n  * `remove()`\n* An Iterator allows us to access elements without directly\ndepending on the internal structure of the collection.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What Happens When iterator() is Called?\n\n* When `iterator()` is called on a collection object, it returns an\nIterator object.\n* The returned object is an implementation of the `Iterator`\ninterface.\n* Different collection classes can have different Iterator\nimplementations.\n* The Iterator object is associated with the collection from which\nit was created.\n* Conceptually, the Iterator maintains a reference to the original\ncollection and maintains information about its current traversal\nposition.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Iterator in ArrayList\n\n* `ArrayList` is an index-based collection.\n* Therefore, its Iterator can use an index internally to maintain\nits position.\n* When the Iterator is created, its cursor starts at position `0`.\n\n### Initial Position\n\nBefore calling `next()` for the first time:\n\n**Cursor = 0**\n\nThe cursor represents the position from which the next element\nwill be returned.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Working of next()\n\nThe `next()` method:\n\n1. Gets the element at the Iterator's current position.\n2. Returns that element.\n3. Moves the Iterator's position forward.\n\n### Conceptually in ArrayList:\n\n* Before first `next()` → cursor = 0\n* First `next()` → returns element at position 0 → cursor\nbecomes 1\n* Second `next()` → returns element at position 1 → cursor\nbecomes 2\n* Third `next()` → returns element at position 2 → cursor\nbecomes 3\n\n### Important\n\n`next()` does two main things:\n\n**Return the next element + Move the Iterator forward**\n\n---\n\n# 5. Working of hasNext()\n\n`hasNext()` checks whether another element is available for\ntraversal.\n\nIt:\n\n* Checks the current Iterator position.\n* Returns `true` if another element is available.\n* Returns `false` if no element is available.\n* Does **not** move the Iterator.\n* Does **not** consume any element.\n\n### Conceptually in ArrayList:\n\nCondition:\n\n**current position < collection size**\n\nIf the condition is true:\n\n**hasNext() → true**\n\nIf the condition is false:\n\n**hasNext() → false**\n\n---\n\n# 6. Relationship Between hasNext() and next()\n\nThe normal Iterator traversal works like this:\n\n**hasNext() → next() → hasNext() → next() → ...**\n\n### Process:\n\n1. `hasNext()` checks whether an element is available.\n2. If it returns `true`, `next()` can be called.\n3. `next()` returns the next element.\n4. `next()` moves the Iterator's position forward.\n5. `hasNext()` checks again from the new position.\n6. This continues until `hasNext()` returns `false`.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Important Difference Between hasNext() and next()\n\n| Method      | Purpose                               | Moves Iterator?\n|\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `hasNext()` | Checks whether another element exists | No\n|\n| `next()`    | Returns the next element              | Yes\n|\n\n### Remember:\n\n**hasNext() = Check**\n\n**next() = Get + Move**\n\n---\n\n# 8. What Happens When There Is No Next Element?\n\n* If `hasNext()` returns `false`, there is no more element\navailable.\n* Calling `next()` when no element is available results in\n`NoSuchElementException`.\n\nTherefore, normally we check `hasNext()` before calling `next()`.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Iterator in Set\n\n* A `Set` does not have indexes.\n* Therefore, its Iterator does not need to use an index.\n* The Iterator uses its own internal mechanism to maintain its\ntraversal position.\n* The exact mechanism depends on the particular Set\nimplementation.\n\nFor example, internally it may keep track of:\n\n* Nodes\n* Buckets\n* References to the next element\n* Other implementation-specific information\n\n---\n\n# 10. Iterator Position vs Index\n\nThis is a very important concept.\n\n### Index\n\nAn index is a numeric position used by index-based collections.\n\nFor example:\n\n**0, 1, 2, 3...**\n\n`ArrayList` supports indexes.\n\n### Iterator Position\n\nIterator position simply means:\n\n**\"Where is the Iterator currently positioned during traversal?\"**\n\nAn Iterator position does **not** have to be represented by a\nnumeric index.\n\nTherefore:\n\n**Iterator position ≠ Index**\n\n---\n\n# 11. How Iterator Works With Different Collections\n\n`Iterator` is an interface.\n\nDifferent collections can provide different Iterator\nimplementations.\n\nConceptually:\n\n* `ArrayList` → ArrayList-specific Iterator\n* `HashSet` → HashSet-specific Iterator\n* `TreeSet` → TreeSet-specific Iterator\n* Other collections → their own appropriate Iterator\nimplementation\n\nEach implementation can use a different mechanism to maintain its\ntraversal position.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Why Does Iterator Need the Collection?\n\nThe Iterator needs to know:\n\n**\"From which collection should I retrieve the next element?\"**\n\nTherefore, the Iterator is associated with the collection from\nwhich it was created.\n\nThe Iterator also needs to maintain its current traversal state\nso that every call to `next()` returns the appropriate next\nelement.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Complete Working Concept\n\n### When Iterator is created:\n\n**Collection → creates/returns Iterator object**\n\nThe Iterator starts at the beginning of the collection's traversal.\n\n### Then:\n\n**hasNext()**\n\n↓\n\nChecks whether another element is available.\n\n↓\n\nIf `true`\n\n↓\n\n**next()**\n\n↓\n\nReturns the next element.\n\n↓\n\nIterator moves forward.\n\n↓\n\n**hasNext()**\n\n↓\n\nChecks again.\n\n↓\n\nThis continues until:\n\n**hasNext() → false**\n\n---\n\n# 14. Key Points for Exams/Interviews\n\n* `Iterator` is an interface.\n* `iterator()` returns an Iterator object.\n* The returned object is an implementation of the Iterator\ninterface.\n* The Iterator is associated with the original collection.\n* `hasNext()` checks whether another element is available.\n* `hasNext()` does not move the Iterator.\n* `next()` returns the next element.\n* `next()` moves the Iterator forward.\n* In an `ArrayList`, the Iterator can use an index internally.\n* In a `Set`, there is no index, so the Iterator uses another\ninternal mechanism.\n* Iterator position does not necessarily mean an index.\n* `hasNext()` means **check**.\n* `next()` means **get + move**.\n* Calling `next()` when no element is available causes\n`NoSuchElementException`.\n\n## One-Line Revision\n\n**Iterator maintains the current traversal position; `hasNext()`\nchecks whether another element exists, while `next()` returns\nthat element and advances the Iterator.**\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "\n\n## 1. What is an Iterator?\n\n* `Iterator` is an interface used to traverse elements of a collection one by one.\n* It provides methods such as:\n\n  * `hasNext()`\n  * `next()`\n  * `remove()`\n* An Iterator allows us to access elements without directly depending on the internal structure of the collection.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What Happens When iterator() is Called?\n\n* When `iterator()` is called on a collection object, it returns an Iterator object.\n* The returned object is an implementation of the `Iterator` interface.\n* Different collection classes can have different Iterator implementations.\n* The Iterator object is associated with the collection from which it was created.\n* Conceptually, the Iterator maintains a reference to the original collection and maintains information about its current traversal position.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Iterator in ArrayList\n\n* `ArrayList` is an index-based collection.\n* Therefore, its Iterator can use an index internally to maintain its position.\n* When the Iterator is created, its cursor starts at position `0`.\n\n### Initial Position\n\nBefore calling `next()` for the first time:\n\n**Cursor = 0**\n\nThe cursor represents the position from which the next element will be returned.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Working of next()\n\nThe `next()` method:\n\n1. Gets the element at the Iterator's current position.\n2. Returns that element.\n3. Moves the Iterator's position forward.\n\n### Conceptually in ArrayList:\n\n* Before first `next()` → cursor = 0\n* First `next()` → returns element at position 0 → cursor becomes 1\n* Second `next()` → returns element at position 1 → cursor becomes 2\n* Third `next()` → returns element at position 2 → cursor becomes 3\n\n### Important\n\n`next()` does two main things:\n\n**Return the next element + Move the Iterator forward**\n\n---\n\n# 5. Working of hasNext()\n\n`hasNext()` checks whether another element is available for traversal.\n\nIt:\n\n* Checks the current Iterator position.\n* Returns `true` if another element is available.\n* Returns `false` if no element is available.\n* Does **not** move the Iterator.\n* Does **not** consume any element.\n\n### Conceptually in ArrayList:\n\nCondition:\n\n**current position < collection size**\n\nIf the condition is true:\n\n**hasNext() → true**\n\nIf the condition is false:\n\n**hasNext() → false**\n\n---\n\n# 6. Relationship Between hasNext() and next()\n\nThe normal Iterator traversal works like this:\n\n**hasNext() → next() → hasNext() → next() → ...**\n\n### Process:\n\n1. `hasNext()` checks whether an element is available.\n2. If it returns `true`, `next()` can be called.\n3. `next()` returns the next element.\n4. `next()` moves the Iterator's position forward.\n5. `hasNext()` checks again from the new position.\n6. This continues until `hasNext()` returns `false`.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Important Difference Between hasNext() and next()\n\n| Method      | Purpose                               | Moves Iterator? |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `hasNext()` | Checks whether another element exists | No              |\n| `next()`    | Returns the next element              | Yes             |\n\n### Remember:\n\n**hasNext() = Check**\n\n**next() = Get + Move**\n\n---\n\n# 8. What Happens When There Is No Next Element?\n\n* If `hasNext()` returns `false`, there is no more element available.\n* Calling `next()` when no element is available results in `NoSuchElementException`.\n\nTherefore, normally we check `hasNext()` before calling `next()`.\n\n---\n\n# 9. Iterator in Set\n\n* A `Set` does not have indexes.\n* Therefore, its Iterator does not need to use an index.\n* The Iterator uses its own internal mechanism to maintain its traversal position.\n* The exact mechanism depends on the particular Set implementation.\n\nFor example, internally it may keep track of:\n\n* Nodes\n* Buckets\n* References to the next element\n* Other implementation-specific information\n\n---\n\n# 10. Iterator Position vs Index\n\nThis is a very important concept.\n\n### Index\n\nAn index is a numeric position used by index-based collections.\n\nFor example:\n\n**0, 1, 2, 3...**\n\n`ArrayList` supports indexes.\n\n### Iterator Position\n\nIterator position simply means:\n\n**\"Where is the Iterator currently positioned during traversal?\"**\n\nAn Iterator position does **not** have to be represented by a numeric index.\n\nTherefore:\n\n**Iterator position ≠ Index**\n\n---\n\n# 11. How Iterator Works With Different Collections\n\n`Iterator` is an interface.\n\nDifferent collections can provide different Iterator implementations.\n\nConceptually:\n\n* `ArrayList` → ArrayList-specific Iterator\n* `HashSet` → HashSet-specific Iterator\n* `TreeSet` → TreeSet-specific Iterator\n* Other collections → their own appropriate Iterator implementation\n\nEach implementation can use a different mechanism to maintain its traversal position.\n\n---\n\n# 12. Why Does Iterator Need the Collection?\n\nThe Iterator needs to know:\n\n**\"From which collection should I retrieve the next element?\"**\n\nTherefore, the Iterator is associated with the collection from which it was created.\n\nThe Iterator also needs to maintain its current traversal state so that every call to `next()` returns the appropriate next element.\n\n---\n\n# 13. Complete Working Concept\n\n### When Iterator is created:\n\n**Collection → creates/returns Iterator object**\n\nThe Iterator starts at the beginning of the collection's traversal.\n\n### Then:\n\n**hasNext()**\n\n↓\n\nChecks whether another element is available.\n\n↓\n\nIf `true`\n\n↓\n\n**next()**\n\n↓\n\nReturns the next element.\n\n↓\n\nIterator moves forward.\n\n↓\n\n**hasNext()**\n\n↓\n\nChecks again.\n\n↓\n\nThis continues until:\n\n**hasNext() → false**\n\n---\n\n# 14. Key Points for Exams/Interviews\n\n* `Iterator` is an interface.\n* `iterator()` returns an Iterator object.\n* The returned object is an implementation of the Iterator interface.\n* The Iterator is associated with the original collection.\n* `hasNext()` checks whether another element is available.\n* `hasNext()` does not move the Iterator.\n* `next()` returns the next element.\n* `next()` moves the Iterator forward.\n* In an `ArrayList`, the Iterator can use an index internally.\n* In a `Set`, there is no index, so the Iterator uses another internal mechanism.\n* Iterator position does not necessarily mean an index.\n* `hasNext()` means **check**.\n* `next()` means **get + move**.\n* Calling `next()` when no element is available causes `NoSuchElementException`.\n\n## One-Line Revision\n\n**Iterator maintains the current traversal position; `hasNext()` checks whether another element exists, while `next()` returns that element and advances the Iterator.**\n",
